canonical_claim.statement:
Exact design in which every school reports completely, the effect of treatment is the same constant in every school, schools are randomized two of four within the block, and the analysis of the schools kept by the rule of the released code does not return that constant (the checker's construction; not in the paper).
[Design] One randomization block of four schools $i = 1,2,3,4$. The set of treated schools is one of the six two-element subsets of $\{1,2,3,4\}$, each with probability $\tfrac16$; $D_i = 1$ if school $i$ is treated and $D_i = 0$ otherwise.
[Potential outcomes, fixed before randomization] The school mean count per student is $Y_i(0) = (0, \tfrac{3}{100}, \tfrac{6}{100}, \tfrac{9}{100})_i$ without treatment and $Y_i(1) = Y_i(0) + \tfrac{3}{100}$ with treatment; the observed mean is $Y_i = Y_i(D_i)$, recorded without error. (With 100 students per school all these means are integer counts divided by 100.)
[Selection rule of the released code] School $i$ is kept when $Y_i > \tfrac{1}{50}$: $A_i = \mathbf{1}\{Y_i > \tfrac1{50}\}$.
Claims.
(i) $Y_i(1) - Y_i(0) = \tfrac{3}{100}$ for every school, so the average effect of treatment is $\tfrac{3}{100}$ in the block and in every subset of its schools.
(ii) School 1 is dropped exactly when it is a control school ($Y_1 = 0$); in every other case every school is kept. So the kept set depends on the assignment.
(iii) For the six assignments, with $n_T$, $n_C$ the numbers of kept treated and kept control schools, $\Delta$ the difference between the mean of $Y$ over the kept treated schools and over the kept control schools, and $w = n_Tn_C/(n_T + n_C)$:
treated $\{1,2\}$: $(n_T,n_C) = (2,2)$, $\Delta = -\tfrac{3}{100}$, $w = 1$;
treated $\{1,3\}$: $(2,2)$, $\Delta = 0$, $w = 1$;
treated $\{1,4\}$: $(2,2)$, $\Delta = \tfrac{3}{100}$, $w = 1$;
treated $\{2,3\}$: $(2,1)$, $\Delta = -\tfrac{3}{200}$, $w = \tfrac23$;
treated $\{2,4\}$: $(2,1)$, $\Delta = \tfrac{3}{100}$, $w = \tfrac23$;
treated $\{3,4\}$: $(2,1)$, $\Delta = \tfrac{3}{40}$, $w = \tfrac23$.
(iv) [Difference in means among the kept schools.] $\mathrm{E}[\Delta] = \tfrac16\cdot\tfrac{9}{100} = \tfrac{3}{200} \ne \tfrac{3}{100}$.
(v) [Regression with a block effect.] In a block with $n_T \ge 1$ kept treated and $n_C \ge 1$ kept control schools, the within-block sum of squares of the treatment indicator is $w = n_Tn_C/(n_T+n_C)$ and its within-block cross-product with the outcome is $w\Delta$; so the OLS coefficient on treatment in the regression of the kept schools' outcomes on block indicators and treatment, over blocks $b = 1,\dots,B$, is $\sum_bw_b\Delta_b/\sum_bw_b$. Here $\mathrm{E}[w] = \tfrac56$ and $\mathrm{E}[w\Delta] = \tfrac{1}{100}$, and for $B$ independent copies of the block the coefficient converges in probability, as $B \to \infty$, to $\tfrac{1/100}{5/6} = \tfrac{3}{250} \ne \tfrac{3}{100}$.
